title: ch2_banker_intro
---
An owl in a tweed vest knocks on your door with a business card. Community bank, Atlantic Avenue. #line:ch2_banker_intro_01
Ms. Lee: I'm Ms. Lee. I lend to small businesses in this neighborhood. I like to meet owners before they need me. #line:ch2_banker_intro_02
-> Invite her in and show her your numbers. #line:ch2_banker_intro_03
    <<choose "meet">>
    Ms. Lee: Clean books. Rare. Call me before you buy your next unit, not after. #line:ch2_banker_intro_04
-> Take the card and say you'll call. #line:ch2_banker_intro_05
    <<choose "later">>
    Ms. Lee: Everyone says that. The smart ones do. #line:ch2_banker_intro_06
===

title: ch2_rosa_intro
---
A hen in steel-toe boots drops a box of samples on your counter and doesn't wait to be asked. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_01
Rosa: Rosa. Supplies, Red Hook. Whatever you sell, somebody is overcharging you for the stuff it's made of. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_02
-> Ask what she can do on price. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_03
    <<choose "listen">>
    Rosa: Pay on time and I'll beat your current supplier. Pay late once and I won't. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_04
-> Say you're happy with your supplier. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_05
    <<choose "busy">>
    Rosa: Nobody's happy with their supplier. Keep the samples. #line:ch2_rosa_intro_06
===

title: ch2_mia_intro
---
A fox with a voice recorder stops you outside. She writes for a Brooklyn neighborhood news site. #line:ch2_mia_intro_01
Mia: Mia. I'm doing a piece on new businesses on this street. Got ten minutes and the truth? #line:ch2_mia_intro_02
-> Tell her the real story, losses included. #line:ch2_mia_intro_03
    <<choose "honest">>
    Mia: Honest founders make the best stories. Readers can smell a brochure. #line:ch2_mia_intro_04
-> Politely decline. Too early for press. #line:ch2_mia_intro_05
    <<choose "decline">>
    Mia: Fair. I'll check back when you're bigger. #line:ch2_mia_intro_06
===

title: ch2_sam_intro
---
Sam, a beaver you studied with, shows up with two coffees and a spreadsheet. #line:ch2_sam_intro_01
Sam: I've watched you do everything alone. I'm good at operations. What if we built this together? #line:ch2_sam_intro_02
-> Hear Sam out over the spreadsheet. #line:ch2_sam_intro_03
    <<choose "talk">>
    Sam: No rush. Think about what you'd want in writing. #line:ch2_sam_intro_04
-> Say you want to stay solo. #line:ch2_sam_intro_05
    <<choose "solo">>
    Sam: Understood. Offer stands if you change your mind. #line:ch2_sam_intro_06
===

title: ch2_block_party
---
Your block is throwing a summer party. The organizers ask local businesses to chip in three hundred dollars. #line:ch2_block_party_01
-> Sponsor it and set up a table. #line:ch2_block_party_02
    <<choose "sponsor">>
    Kids in face paint, neighbors with your flyers. Good afternoon. #line:ch2_block_party_03
-> Skip it and work. #line:ch2_block_party_04
    <<choose "skip">>
    You hear the music from the shop. Nobody comes in. #line:ch2_block_party_05
===

title: ch2_mom_visit
---
Mom: So this is the famous Brooklyn studio. It's small. Is the business doing well, or are you just saying that? #line:ch2_mom_visit_01
-> Close early and take her to dinner. #line:ch2_mom_visit_02
    <<choose "dinner">>
    Mom: You look tired, but proud. That's how your grandfather looked. #line:ch2_mom_visit_03
-> Show her the numbers, then get back to work. #line:ch2_mom_visit_04
    <<choose "work">>
    Mom: Numbers are nice. Call me sometime without them. #line:ch2_mom_visit_05
===

title: boss_fatcat_price_war
---
A banner goes up across the street. Same business as yours, twenty percent cheaper. Grand opening. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_01
Mr. Whiskerton: Remember me? Your old landlord. I liked your idea so much I bought one too. Let's see who lasts. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_02
Unpaid CEO: He can lose money longer than you can. Don't fight him where he's strongest. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_03
-> Ignore him and change nothing. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_04
    <<choose "ignore">>
    For three months his banner is louder than your quality. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_05
-> Match his prices. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_06
    <<choose "match">>
    Customers stay. Your margin goes to war instead of you. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_07
-> Compete on service his chain can't copy. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_08
    <<choose "different">>
    Unpaid CEO: Price is a race to the bottom. Loyalty is a race he doesn't know how to run. #line:boss_fatcat_price_war_09
===
